select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '825773c58568', 11, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.0", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nIs a share more likely to finish above its expected price, or below?\nNorthwind shares trade at $28. Investors expect a return of 12 percent per year, so that the expected price in one year is \\(28e^{0.12}=\\$31.57\\). The volatility is 39 percent. The figure shows the distribution of the price in one year under the model of this chapter. Before the expected price is marked on it, select the statement that is correct."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.1#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.1", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.1", "heading": "The Markov property", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.1 The Markov property", "body": "A variable whose value changes over time in an uncertain way is said to follow a stochastic process. The process is discrete-time if the value can change only at certain fixed dates, as on a binomial tree, and continuous-time if it can change at any moment. It is a discrete-variable process if only certain values are possible and a continuous-variable process if any value within a range is possible. Share prices are in reality quoted in discrete units and change only when the market is open, but a continuous-variable, continuous-time process describes them well and is far more convenient mathematically."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.1", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.1", "heading": "The Markov property", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.1 The Markov property", "body": "A Markov process is a stochastic process in which only the current value of the variable is relevant for predicting its future. The past history of the variable, and the path by which the present value was reached, add nothing. Share prices are assumed to follow a Markov process. The assumption is the weak form of market efficiency: the present price of a share already reflects all the information contained in the record of its past prices. If it did not, traders who studied past prices could earn more than a fair return, and their trading would remove the pattern on which they relied.\nThe Markov property concerns probabilities and not certainties. It asserts that the probability distribution of the price at any future date depends only on the price today."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.2#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.2", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.2", "heading": "The Wiener process", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.2 The Wiener process", "body": "Consider a variable that follows a Markov process and whose change over one year is normally distributed with a mean of zero and a variance of 1. What is the distribution of its change over two years? The change over two years is the sum of two changes of one year each, and by the Markov property the two are independent. The mean of the sum is zero, and the variances of independent variables add, so the variance is 2 and the standard deviation \\(\\sqrt2\\). By the same argument the change over six months has a variance of 0.5, and the change over any interval of length \\(T\\) has a variance of \\(T\\) and a standard deviation of \\(\\sqrt{T}\\).\nThis is the essential arithmetic of uncertainty over time. Variances are proportional to time; standard deviations are proportional to the square root of time. Uncertainty over four years is twice, and not four times, uncertainty over one year.\nThe process just described is a Wiener process, also called Brownian motion. A variable \\(z\\) follows a Wiener process if it has two properties.\nDefinition 11.1 — Wiener process"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.2#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.2", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.2", "heading": "The Wiener process", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.2 The Wiener process", "body": "Property 1. The change \\(\\Delta z\\) during a short interval of length \\(\\Delta t\\) is \\[ \\Delta z=\\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\Delta t}, \\] where \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a random draw from the standard normal distribution, which has mean 0 and standard deviation 1. Property 2. The values of \\(\\Delta z\\) for any two different short intervals are independent.\nProperty 1 implies that \\(\\Delta z\\) is normally distributed with mean zero and standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\). Property 2 is the Markov property. Together they imply that the change in \\(z\\) over any interval of length \\(T\\) is normal with mean zero and standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{T}\\).\nIn the limit as \\(\\Delta t\\) approaches zero, the change is written \\(dz\\). The path of a Wiener process is continuous but extremely irregular: because \\(\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\) is much larger than \\(\\Delta t\\) when \\(\\Delta t\\) is small, the variable changes direction incessantly, and the length of the path it traces in any interval is infinite."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.3#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.3", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.3", "heading": "The generalized Wiener process", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.3 The generalized Wiener process", "body": "A Wiener process has an expected change of zero and a variance of 1 per year. A generalized Wiener process allows any constant expected change and any constant variance. A variable \\(x\\) follows a generalized Wiener process if\n\\[ dx=a\\,dt+b\\,dz, \\]\nwhere \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants. The term \\(a\\,dt\\) states that \\(x\\) is expected to increase by \\(a\\) per unit of time; \\(a\\) is the drift rate. The term \\(b\\,dz\\) adds variability equal to \\(b\\) times a Wiener process; \\(b^{2}\\) is the variance rate. Over a short interval \\(\\Delta t\\),\n\\[ \\Delta x=a\\,\\Delta t+b\\,\\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\Delta t}, \\]\nand over any interval of length \\(T\\) the change in \\(x\\) is normally distributed with mean \\(aT\\) and standard deviation \\(b\\sqrt{T}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.3#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.3", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.3", "heading": "The generalized Wiener process", "kind": "example", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.3 The generalized Wiener process", "body": "Worked example 11.1 — A generalized Wiener process\nThe cash balance of a company, in millions of dollars, follows a generalized Wiener process with a drift rate of 20 per year and a variance rate of 900 per year. The balance is now 50. Find the distribution of the balance in six months and in one year.\n1\nParameters. \\(a=20\\); \\(b=\\sqrt{900}=30\\).\n2\nSix months. Mean: \\(50+20\\times0.5=60\\). Standard deviation: \\(30\\sqrt{0.5}=21.2\\). The balance is normally distributed with mean 60 and standard deviation 21.2.\n3\nOne year. Mean: \\(50+20=70\\). Standard deviation: \\(30\\sqrt{1}=30\\).\n4\nObservation. Doubling the horizon doubles the expected change, from 10 to 20, but multiplies the standard deviation only by \\(\\sqrt2\\), from 21.2 to 30."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.3#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.3", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.3", "heading": "The generalized Wiener process", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.3 The generalized Wiener process", "body": "A further generalization allows the drift and variance rates to depend on the value of the variable and on time. A process of the form\n\\[ dx=a(x,t)\\,dt+b(x,t)\\,dz \\]\nis an Itô process. Over a short interval the change is \\(\\Delta x=a(x,t)\\Delta t+b(x,t)\\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\), on the assumption that the drift and variance rates remain at their values at the start of the interval."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.4#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.4", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.4", "heading": "The process for a share price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.4 The process for a share price", "body": "A generalized Wiener process with constant drift and variance would be a poor model of a share price. It would imply that the expected change in the price is the same whether the price is $10 or $100, whereas investors require the same expected percentage return whatever the price; and it would permit the price to become negative. The appropriate assumption is that the expected return and the variability of the return are constant.\nResult 11.1 — Geometric Brownian motion\nThe price \\(S\\) of a share that pays no dividend is assumed to follow the process \\[ dS=\\mu S\\,dt+\\sigma S\\,dz, \\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad \\frac{dS}{S}=\\mu\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dz, \\] where \\(\\mu\\) is the expected rate of return on the share per year and \\(\\sigma\\) is the volatility of the share price per year. Over a short interval, \\[ \\frac{\\Delta S}{S}=\\mu\\,\\Delta t+\\sigma\\,\\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\Delta t}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.4#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.4", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.4", "heading": "The process for a share price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.4 The process for a share price", "body": "This process is geometric Brownian motion. The return \\(\\Delta S/S\\) over a short interval is normally distributed with mean \\(\\mu\\,\\Delta t\\) and standard deviation \\(\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\). The model is the limit of the binomial tree of Result 10.2 as the length of the step approaches zero: on that tree the return in each step is \\(+\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\) or \\(-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\), approximately, and the sum of a large number of such steps is normally distributed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.4#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.4", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.4", "heading": "The process for a share price", "kind": "example", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.4 The process for a share price", "body": "Worked example 11.2 — One week of Northwind's share price\nNorthwind shares trade at $28, with \\(\\mu=0.12\\) and \\(\\sigma=0.39\\). Find the distribution of the change in the price over one week, and the change that results from a random draw of \\(\\varepsilon=-1.20\\).\n1\nInterval. \\(\\Delta t=1/52=0.01923\\); \\(\\sqrt{\\Delta t}=0.13868\\).\n2\nReturn. Mean: \\(0.12\\times0.01923=0.00231\\). Standard deviation: \\(0.39\\times0.13868=0.05408\\).\n3\nPrice change. Mean: \\(28\\times0.00231=\\$0.065\\). Standard deviation: \\(28\\times0.05408=\\$1.514\\).\n4\nA particular outcome. \\(\\Delta S=28\\times(0.00231+0.05408\\times(-1.20))=28\\times(-0.06259)=-\\$1.75\\); the price falls to $26.25.\n5\nObservation. Over one week the standard deviation of the change is more than twenty times its mean. Over short intervals the behavior of a share price is dominated by its volatility; the expected return becomes visible only over years."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.4#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.4", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.4", "heading": "The process for a share price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.4 The process for a share price", "body": "Repeating the calculation of step 4 week after week, each time with a new random draw and the price reached in the previous week, produces a simulated path of the share price. This procedure is Monte Carlo simulation, and Chapter 16 uses it to value derivatives. Interactive figure 11.1 displays twenty such paths."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.4#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.4", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.4", "heading": "The process for a share price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.4 The process for a share price", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 11.1 — Simulated paths of a share price\nTwenty paths of geometric Brownian motion\nEach grey line is one possible future of a share now priced at $28, simulated in weekly steps. The solid line is the expected price, the dashed line the median, and the dotted lines the limits within which the price lies with a probability of 95 percent. Vary the expected return and the volatility, and generate new paths. Observe which of the two parameters governs the appearance of the paths."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.5#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.5", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.5", "heading": "The lognormal distribution of share prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.5 The lognormal distribution of share prices", "body": "Result 11.1 describes the change in the price over a short interval. For the valuation of options the distribution of the price at a future date \\(T\\) is required. The following result is derived in Section 11.6.\nResult 11.2 — The lognormal property\nIf a share price follows geometric Brownian motion, the natural logarithm of the price at time \\(T\\) is normally distributed: \\[ \\ln S_T\\ \\sim\\ \\phi\\!\\left[\\,\\ln S_0+\\left(\\mu-\\frac{\\sigma^{2}}{2}\\right)T,\\ \\ \\sigma^{2}T\\,\\right], \\] where \\(\\phi[m,v]\\) denotes a normal distribution with mean \\(m\\) and variance \\(v\\).\nA variable whose logarithm is normally distributed has a lognormal distribution. A lognormal variable can take any value between zero and infinity, and its distribution is skewed: the mean, the median, and the most likely value are all different, as the opening exercise showed. From the properties of the lognormal distribution,\n\\[ E(S_T)=S_0\\,e^{\\mu T}, \\qquad \\text{median}(S_T)=S_0\\,e^{(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2)T}, \\qquad \\operatorname{var}(S_T)=S_0^{2}\\,e^{2\\mu T}\\left(e^{\\sigma^{2}T}-1\\right). \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.5#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.5", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.5", "heading": "The lognormal distribution of share prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.5 The lognormal distribution of share prices", "body": "The expected price grows at the rate \\(\\mu\\), in accordance with the definition of \\(\\mu\\) as the expected return. The median grows at the lower rate \\(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2\\). For Northwind over one year, the expected price is \\(28e^{0.12}=\\$31.57\\) and the median is \\(28e^{0.12-0.07605}=28e^{0.04395}=\\$29.26\\). The probability that the price exceeds its expected value is \\(N(-\\sigma\\sqrt{T}/2)=N(-0.195)=42.3\\) percent, where \\(N(x)\\) denotes the probability that a standard normal variable is less than \\(x\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.5#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.5", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.5", "heading": "The lognormal distribution of share prices", "kind": "example", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.5 The lognormal distribution of share prices", "body": "Worked example 11.3 — A confidence interval for the share price\nFor Northwind (\\(S_0=28\\), \\(\\mu=0.12\\), \\(\\sigma=0.39\\)), find the 95 percent confidence interval for the share price in six months, and the probability that the price will then exceed $30.\n1\nMean of \\(\\ln S_T\\). \\(\\ln28+(0.12-0.39^{2}/2)\\times0.5=3.3322+0.04395\\times0.5=3.3542\\).\n2\nStandard deviation of \\(\\ln S_T\\). \\(0.39\\sqrt{0.5}=0.2758\\).\n3\nInterval for \\(\\ln S_T\\). A normal variable lies within 1.96 standard deviations of its mean with a probability of 95 percent: \\(3.3542\\pm1.96\\times0.2758\\), that is, from 2.8137 to 3.8947.\n4\nInterval for \\(S_T\\). \\(e^{2.8137}=\\$16.67\\) to \\(e^{3.8947}=\\$49.14\\). The interval is not symmetric about the current price: it extends $11.33 below and $21.14 above.\n5\nProbability of exceeding $30. \\(\\ln30=3.4012\\). The number of standard deviations above the mean is \\((3.4012-3.3542)/0.2758=0.1704\\), and \\(1-N(0.1704)=43.2\\) percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.5#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.5", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.5", "heading": "The distribution of the rate of return", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.5 The distribution of the rate of return", "body": "Let \\(x\\) be the continuously compounded rate of return per year realized between time 0 and time \\(T\\), defined by \\(S_T=S_0e^{xT}\\), so that \\(x=\\ln(S_T/S_0)/T\\). From Result 11.2,\n\\[ x\\ \\sim\\ \\phi\\!\\left[\\,\\mu-\\frac{\\sigma^{2}}{2},\\ \\ \\frac{\\sigma^{2}}{T}\\,\\right]. \\]\nThe expected continuously compounded return is \\(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2\\) and not \\(\\mu\\). For Northwind it is \\(0.12-0.07605=4.4\\) percent per year, against an expected return of 12 percent. Both statements are correct, and they answer different questions. The quantity \\(\\mu\\) is the expected return over a very short period, expressed per year; it is the arithmetic average of the returns over many short periods. The quantity \\(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2\\) is the return, compounded over the whole period, that an investor should expect to realize; it corresponds to the geometric average. The geometric average of a set of unequal numbers is always below the arithmetic average. A share that rises by 50 percent and then falls by 50 percent has an arithmetic average return of zero and has lost a quarter of its value."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.5#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.5", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.5", "heading": "The distribution of the rate of return", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.5 The distribution of the rate of return", "body": "The standard deviation of \\(x\\), \\(\\sigma/\\sqrt{T}\\), declines as the period lengthens: the average annual return over twenty years is known with far greater confidence than the return over one year.\nConcept check 11.1\nA share has a volatility of 30 percent per year. What is the standard deviation of its return over one trading day, taking a year to contain 252 trading days?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.5#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.5", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.5", "heading": "The distribution of the rate of return", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.5 The distribution of the rate of return", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 11.2 — Three-dimensional view\nThe distribution of the share price at every horizon\nThe surface shows the probability density of the Northwind share price at horizons from one month to three years. At short horizons the distribution is tall and narrow about $28; as the horizon lengthens it spreads and becomes skewed. The gold curve is the density at the selected horizon and the marker its median. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.6#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.6", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.6", "heading": "★ Itô's lemma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.6 ★ Itô's lemma", "body": "Optional section. The price of a derivative is a function of the price of the underlying asset and of time. Itô's lemma gives the process followed by such a function. This section uses partial derivatives: \\(\\partial G/\\partial x\\) denotes the rate of change of a function \\(G\\) with respect to \\(x\\) when its other arguments are held fixed.\nResult 11.3 — Itô's lemma\nSuppose that \\(x\\) follows the Itô process \\(dx=a(x,t)\\,dt+b(x,t)\\,dz\\), and let \\(G\\) be a function of \\(x\\) and \\(t\\). Then \\(G\\) follows the process \\[ dG=\\left(\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial x}\\,a+\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial t}+\\frac12\\,\\frac{\\partial^{2}G}{\\partial x^{2}}\\,b^{2}\\right)dt+\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial x}\\,b\\,dz, \\] in which \\(dz\\) is the same Wiener process as in the process for \\(x\\).\nOutline of the derivation. In ordinary calculus a small change in \\(G\\) is approximated by \\(\\Delta G\\approx(\\partial G/\\partial x)\\Delta x+(\\partial G/\\partial t)\\Delta t\\), the terms of higher order being negligible. For an Itô process this is not sufficient. Expanding to the second order,"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.6#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.6", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.6", "heading": "★ Itô's lemma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.6 ★ Itô's lemma", "body": "\\[ \\Delta G=\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial x}\\Delta x+\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial t}\\Delta t+\\frac12\\frac{\\partial^{2}G}{\\partial x^{2}}(\\Delta x)^{2}+\\cdots \\]\nWith \\(\\Delta x=a\\,\\Delta t+b\\,\\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\), the square is \\((\\Delta x)^{2}=b^{2}\\varepsilon^{2}\\Delta t+\\text{terms of higher order in }\\Delta t\\). This term is of the same order as \\(\\Delta t\\) and cannot be neglected. Moreover, the variance of \\(\\varepsilon^{2}\\Delta t\\) is of order \\((\\Delta t)^{2}\\), so that as \\(\\Delta t\\) approaches zero the term ceases to be random and equals its expected value, \\(b^{2}\\Delta t\\), because \\(E(\\varepsilon^{2})=1\\). Substituting \\((\\Delta x)^{2}=b^{2}\\,dt\\) and \\(\\Delta x=a\\,dt+b\\,dz\\) into the expansion gives the lemma. The essential point is expressed in the rule \\((dz)^{2}=dt\\): the square of the random term is not negligible, and it is not random.\nFor a share price, \\(a=\\mu S\\) and \\(b=\\sigma S\\), and the lemma becomes\n\\[ dG=\\left(\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial S}\\,\\mu S+\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial t}+\\frac12\\,\\frac{\\partial^{2}G}{\\partial S^{2}}\\,\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\right)dt+\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial S}\\,\\sigma S\\,dz . \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.6#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.6", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.6", "heading": "★ Itô's lemma", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.6 ★ Itô's lemma", "body": "Both \\(S\\) and \\(G\\) are driven by the same source of uncertainty, \\(dz\\). This fact is the basis of the derivation of the Black–Scholes–Merton equation in Section 12.3: a portfolio of the share and the derivative can be formed in which the two random terms cancel."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.6#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.6", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.6", "heading": "★ Itô's lemma", "kind": "example", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.6 ★ Itô's lemma", "body": "Worked example 11.4 — Two applications of Itô's lemma\nA share price follows \\(dS=\\mu S\\,dt+\\sigma S\\,dz\\). Find the process followed by (a) the forward price \\(F=Se^{r(T-t)}\\) of Result 4.1 and (b) \\(G=\\ln S\\).\n1\n(a) Derivatives. \\(\\partial F/\\partial S=e^{r(T-t)}\\); \\(\\partial^{2}F/\\partial S^{2}=0\\); \\(\\partial F/\\partial t=-rSe^{r(T-t)}\\).\n2\n(a) Process. \\(dF=\\left[e^{r(T-t)}\\mu S-rSe^{r(T-t)}\\right]dt+e^{r(T-t)}\\sigma S\\,dz=(\\mu-r)F\\,dt+\\sigma F\\,dz\\). The forward price follows geometric Brownian motion with the same volatility as the share and an expected growth rate of \\(\\mu-r\\): the excess of the expected return over the risk-free rate.\n3\n(b) Derivatives. \\(\\partial G/\\partial S=1/S\\); \\(\\partial^{2}G/\\partial S^{2}=-1/S^{2}\\); \\(\\partial G/\\partial t=0\\).\n4\n(b) Process. \\(dG=\\left[\\dfrac1S\\,\\mu S-\\dfrac12\\,\\dfrac{1}{S^{2}}\\,\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\right]dt+\\dfrac1S\\,\\sigma S\\,dz=\\left(\\mu-\\dfrac{\\sigma^{2}}{2}\\right)dt+\\sigma\\,dz\\).\n5"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.6#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.6", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.6", "heading": "★ Itô's lemma", "kind": "example", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.6 ★ Itô's lemma", "body": "Conclusion. \\(\\ln S\\) follows a generalized Wiener process with constant drift \\(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2\\) and constant variance rate \\(\\sigma^{2}\\). By Section 11.3, its change between 0 and \\(T\\) is normal with mean \\((\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2)T\\) and variance \\(\\sigma^{2}T\\). This is Result 11.2. The term \\(-\\sigma^{2}/2\\), which distinguishes the median from the mean, arises from the second derivative in the lemma."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.7", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.7", "heading": "Estimating volatility from historical data", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.7 Estimating volatility from historical data", "body": "The volatility of a share can be estimated from a record of its past prices observed at regular intervals: daily, weekly, or monthly. Let \\(S_0,S_1,\\dots,S_n\\) be \\(n+1\\) successive prices, and \\(\\tau\\) the length of the interval between observations, in years. The procedure is as follows.\nCompute the continuously compounded return in each interval: \\(u_i=\\ln(S_i/S_{i-1})\\), for \\(i=1,\\dots,n\\).\nCompute the standard deviation of the \\(u_i\\): \\(s=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(u_i-\\bar u)^{2}}\\), where \\(\\bar u\\) is their mean.\nBy Result 11.2, the standard deviation of \\(u_i\\) is \\(\\sigma\\sqrt{\\tau}\\). The estimate of the volatility is therefore \\(\\hat\\sigma=s/\\sqrt{\\tau}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.7#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.7", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.7", "heading": "Estimating volatility from historical data", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.7 Estimating volatility from historical data", "body": "The standard error of the estimate is approximately \\(\\hat\\sigma/\\sqrt{2n}\\). More observations give greater precision, but volatility changes over time and data from the distant past may no longer be relevant; a common compromise is to use daily closing prices for the most recent 90 to 180 days. When daily data are used, \\(\\tau\\) is taken as \\(1/252\\), the reciprocal of the number of trading days in a year, and not as \\(1/365\\). Evidence shows that volatility is far higher when markets are open than when they are closed, so that it is trading days and not calendar days that should be counted. If a dividend is paid during an interval, the return for that interval is computed as \\(\\ln[(S_i+D)/S_{i-1}]\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.7#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.7", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.7", "heading": "Estimating volatility from historical data", "kind": "example", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.7 Estimating volatility from historical data", "body": "Worked example 11.5 — Estimating volatility from weekly prices\nThe closing price of a share on eleven successive Fridays is $28.00, 28.90, 27.60, 28.40, 29.90, 29.10, 30.60, 29.80, 28.50, 29.70, and 30.40. No dividend was paid. Estimate the volatility.\n1\nReturns. \\(u_1=\\ln(28.90/28.00)=0.03164\\); the ten returns are 0.03164, −0.04603, 0.02857, 0.05147, −0.02712, 0.05026, −0.02649, −0.04460, 0.04124, and 0.02330.\n2\nSums. \\(\\sum u_i=0.08224\\), so \\(\\bar u=0.008224\\). \\(\\sum u_i^{2}=0.014782\\).\n3\nStandard deviation. \\(s=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.014782-10\\times0.008224^{2}}{9}}=\\sqrt{0.0015673}=0.03959\\) per week.\n4\nVolatility. \\(\\hat\\sigma=0.03959/\\sqrt{1/52}=0.03959\\times7.2111=0.2855\\), or 28.5 percent per year.\n5\nPrecision. Standard error: \\(0.2855/\\sqrt{20}=0.064\\). With ten observations the estimate is imprecise: the true volatility could plausibly lie anywhere between 16 and 41 percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.7#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.7", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.7", "heading": "Estimating volatility from historical data", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.7 Estimating volatility from historical data", "body": "The expected return \\(\\mu\\) cannot be estimated in this way with any useful precision. Section 11.5 showed that the average realized return over \\(T\\) years has a standard deviation of \\(\\sigma/\\sqrt{T}\\); with a volatility of 39 percent, even fifty years of data leave a standard deviation of 5.5 percentage points. It is one of the most fortunate facts in the theory of derivatives that, as Chapter 10 showed and Chapter 12 confirms, the value of an option does not depend on \\(\\mu\\) at all.\nConcept check 11.2\nA share has an expected return of 15 percent and a volatility of 40 percent. What continuously compounded return per year should an investor expect to realize over a long holding period?\nConcept check 11.3\nThe standard deviation of the change in a variable that follows a Wiener process is 2.0 over one year. What is it over four years?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.7#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.7", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.7", "heading": "Estimating volatility from historical data", "kind": "notes", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.7 Estimating volatility from historical data", "body": "Historical note\nThe irregular motion of pollen grains suspended in water was described by the botanist Robert Brown in 1827. The first mathematical model of such motion was constructed not by a physicist but by a student of finance: Louis Bachelier, in a doctoral thesis on the Paris bond market submitted in 1900, five years before Albert Einstein's paper on the same subject. Bachelier assumed that price changes, and not returns, were normally distributed, so that his model permitted negative prices; the replacement of the price by its logarithm was proposed by Paul Samuelson in 1965 and gives the geometric Brownian motion of this chapter.\nThe rigorous theory of the process is due to Norbert Wiener, in 1923, and the calculus that bears his name was developed by Kiyosi Itô in Japan in the 1940s. Itô regarded his work as pure mathematics and is reported to have been surprised, late in his life, to learn that it had become the working tool of financial markets."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.summary#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.summary", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.summary Summary", "body": "Share prices are assumed to follow a Markov process: the distribution of future prices depends only on the present price.\nA Wiener process has changes that are independent and normal with mean zero and variance equal to the length of the interval. Variance is proportional to time and standard deviation to its square root.\nA share price is assumed to follow geometric Brownian motion, \\(dS=\\mu S\\,dt+\\sigma S\\,dz\\): over a short interval the return is normal with mean \\(\\mu\\,\\Delta t\\) and standard deviation \\(\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\).\nThe price at a future date is lognormal: \\(\\ln S_T\\) is normal with mean \\(\\ln S_0+(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2)T\\) and variance \\(\\sigma^{2}T\\). The expected price is \\(S_0e^{\\mu T}\\) and the median \\(S_0e^{(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2)T}\\).\nThe expected continuously compounded return is \\(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2\\), which is below the expected return \\(\\mu\\).\nVolatility is estimated as the standard deviation of the logarithmic returns divided by the square root of the interval between observations, in years.\n★ Itô's lemma gives the process followed by a function of a variable that follows an Itô process; its distinctive term is \\(\\tfrac12(\\partial^{2}G/\\partial x^{2})b^{2}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.exercises Exercises", "body": "11.1 A variable follows a generalized Wiener process with a drift rate of 4 per year and a variance rate of 25 per year. Its present value is 30. Find the probability that it is below 30 after two years.\nAfter two years the variable is normal with mean \\(30+8=38\\) and standard deviation \\(5\\sqrt2=7.071\\). The value 30 is \\((30-38)/7.071=-1.131\\) standard deviations from the mean; \\(N(-1.131)=12.9\\) percent.\n11.2 A share trades at $60, with an expected return of 10 percent and a volatility of 25 percent. Find the mean and standard deviation of the change in its price over one trading day (1/252 of a year).\nMean: \\(60\\times0.10/252=\\$0.024\\). Standard deviation: \\(60\\times0.25\\times\\sqrt{1/252}=60\\times0.015749=\\$0.945\\).\n11.3 For the share of Exercise 11.2, find the expected price, the median price, and the 95 percent confidence interval for the price in two years.\nExpected: \\(60e^{0.20}=\\$73.28\\). Mean of \\(\\ln S_T\\): \\(\\ln60+(0.10-0.03125)\\times2=4.0943+0.1375=4.2318\\); median \\(e^{4.2318}=\\$68.84\\). Standard deviation of \\(\\ln S_T\\): \\(0.25\\sqrt2=0.3536\\). Interval: \\(e^{4.2318\\pm1.96\\times0.3536}\\), from \\(e^{3.5388}=\\$34.43\\) to \\(e^{4.9248}=\\$137.66\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.exercises Exercises", "body": "11.4 For the share of Exercise 11.2, find the probability that a one-year European call with a strike of $70 will be exercised.\n\\(\\ln S_T\\) is normal with mean \\(4.0943+0.06875=4.1631\\) and standard deviation 0.25. \\(\\ln70=4.2485\\), which is \\((4.2485-4.1631)/0.25=0.3416\\) standard deviations above the mean. Probability: \\(1-N(0.3416)=36.6\\) percent. This is the probability in the real world; the risk-neutral probability, which enters the valuation of the option, replaces \\(\\mu\\) by \\(r\\).\n11.5 A share's volatility is 32 percent. An analyst states that the standard deviation of its return over one month is \\(32/12=2.7\\) percent. Correct the statement.\nStandard deviations scale with the square root of time: \\(0.32\\sqrt{1/12}=0.32\\times0.2887=9.2\\) percent. Dividing by 12 would be correct for the variance: \\(0.32^{2}/12=0.00853\\), whose square root is 9.2 percent.\n11.6 Daily closing prices of a share on six successive trading days are $50.00, 50.60, 49.90, 50.40, 51.50, and 51.00. Estimate the volatility per year."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.exercises Exercises", "body": "Returns: 0.011929, −0.013931, 0.009970, 0.021591, −0.009756. Mean: 0.003961. Sum of squared deviations: \\(0.000997-5\\times0.003961^{2}=0.000919\\). \\(s=\\sqrt{0.000919/4}=0.01515\\) per day. \\(\\hat\\sigma=0.01515\\times\\sqrt{252}=0.241\\), or 24.1 percent. With five observations the standard error is \\(0.241/\\sqrt{10}=7.6\\) percentage points.\n11.7 A fund reports that over ten years its annual returns averaged 9.0 percent with a standard deviation of 20 percent. Approximately what compounded annual return did an investor realize, and why does it differ from 9.0 percent?\nApproximately \\(0.09-0.20^{2}/2=7.0\\) percent. The reported figure is an arithmetic average. The realized compounded return corresponds to the geometric average, which is lower by about half the variance, because gains and losses of equal percentage size do not offset: a loss of 20 percent requires a gain of 25 percent to recover.\n11.8 ★ A share price follows \\(dS=\\mu S\\,dt+\\sigma S\\,dz\\). Use Itô's lemma to find the process followed by \\(G=S^{2}\\), and state the expected growth rate and volatility of \\(G\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.exercises", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(\\partial G/\\partial S=2S\\); \\(\\partial^{2}G/\\partial S^{2}=2\\); \\(\\partial G/\\partial t=0\\). \\(dG=\\left[2S\\,\\mu S+\\tfrac12\\times2\\times\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\right]dt+2S\\,\\sigma S\\,dz=(2\\mu+\\sigma^{2})G\\,dt+2\\sigma G\\,dz\\). \\(G\\) follows geometric Brownian motion with an expected growth rate of \\(2\\mu+\\sigma^{2}\\) and a volatility of \\(2\\sigma\\). Ordinary calculus would give a growth rate of \\(2\\mu\\); the additional \\(\\sigma^{2}\\) is the contribution of the second-derivative term."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:11.further-reading#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:11.further-reading", "chapter": 11, "section": "11.further-reading", "heading": "Further reading", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 11, Stochastic Processes and Itô's Lemma — 11.further-reading Further reading", "body": "Bachelier, L. (1900). Théorie de la spéculation. Annales Scientifiques de l'École Normale Supérieure, 17, 21–86.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapters 14–15.\nItô, K. (1951). On stochastic differential equations. Memoirs of the American Mathematical Society, 4, 1–51.\nNeftci, S. N. (2000). An Introduction to the Mathematics of Financial Derivatives (2nd ed.). Academic Press.\nSamuelson, P. A. (1965). Rational theory of warrant pricing. Industrial Management Review, 6(2), 13–31."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
